ceous unit. It classically presents as a 1–2 cm dome-shaped lesion and commonly affects older adults in sun-exposed areas, such as the face, neck, and dorsum of the extremities. A keratoacanthoma is most often a solitary, pink or flesh-colored dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin plug developing on the sun-exposed skin of elderly persons. Sep 19, 2025 · Keratoacanthoma (KA) is a cutaneous tumor that most commonly presents as a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (picture 1A-E) [1,2]. KA most frequently develops on hair-bearing, sun-exposed skin. Keratoacanthoma is a rapidly growing, dome-shaped skin lesion with a central keratin plug, typically arising in sun-damaged skin of older adults. While often benign and self-limiting, it is usually managed with surgical excision due to its resemblance to squamous cell carcinoma. Keratoacanthomas are rapidly growing, dome-shaped skin tumors with a central keratin-filled crater. They most commonly arise on sun-exposed areas in older adults (1)."}, {"title": "Keratoacanthoma in Older Adults | Springer Nature Link", "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-53888-9_66-1", "snippet": "Nov 28, 2025 · Keratoacanthoma is a benign epithelial skin tumor that originates from the pilosebaceous unit. It classically presents as a 1–2 cm dome-shaped lesion and commonly affects older adults in sun-exposed areas, such as the face, neck, and dorsum of the extremities."}, {"title": "Keratoacanthoma: Symptoms, Causes & Treatment - Cleveland Clinic", "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/keratoacanthoma", "snippet": "Nov 18, 2025 · A keratoacanthoma is a fast-growing, dome-shaped bump that often has a scab-like plug in the center. It usually appears on skin that gets a lot of sun, like your face, neck, arms or hands. It’s pronounced “ker-a-to-ac-an-tho-ma.” This tumor is usually noncancerous and often goes away on its own."}]}
